{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's claim that the expert will outperform fails for a central class of objectively verifiable tasks: those where individual application of the verification procedure is high-variance or error-prone (e.g., visual detection tasks, prolonged multi-step calculations). A non-expert panel applying even a 'lossy compressed' checklist can achieve higher reliability through aggregation canceling individual noise, while a single expert—no matter how well they internalized the verification logic—is subject to a single-instance variance the panel averages out.","counterexample":[{"task":"Objectively verifiable diagnostic task with a known checklist","expert_internalized_logic_accuracy":"~85% (high mean, subject to single-trial fatigue/variance)","panel_checklist_accuracy":"~92% (aggregation of many 70% individual non-experts cancels noise)","result":"Panel outperforms single expert despite using lossy compressed checklist"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's core undecidability claim asserts that 'genuine reasoning' and 'mere compliance' make identical point predictions for aggregate accuracy, rendering them inseparable. But this collapses a crucial distinction: genuine reasoning generalizes to cases outside the checklist's explicit coverage. A single evaluation can include a held-out probe set of novel cases outside the checklist's scope; if the panel's accuracy on these probe cases significantly exceeds the pure baseline expected from blind checklist compliance (which would predict near-chance or systematic failure on novel cases), genuine reasoning is demonstrated. Thus, the hypotheses do not make identical point predictions across a properly partitioned evaluation set.","counterexample":[{"evaluation_design":"A single evaluation containing both standard checklist-covered cases and a held-out block of novel edge-cases outside checklist coverage","panel_compliance_prediction":"High accuracy on covered cases, but drops to chance/baseline on novel edge-cases","panel_reasoning_prediction":"High accuracy on covered cases, AND maintains above-baseline accuracy on novel edge-cases by reasoning through them","result":"The two hypotheses make separable point predictions across the partitioned evaluation, refuting undecidability"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 identifies a genuine reliability gain merely by showing the expert has systematic bias while the panel does not. However, this conflates 'genuine reliability gain over a flawed expert' with 'genuine reasoning by the panel.' If the panel avoids the expert's bias strictly by blindly following a checklist that mechanically prevents it, the panel's superior reliability is merely compliance-driven, not a genuine reasoning gain. SRC_003 fails to distinguish compliance that happens to avoid the expert's bias from genuine reasoning, which is the exact distinction the problem demands.","counterexample":[{"expert":"Systematically overconfident, 70% accuracy","panel":"Blindly follows a checklist with an explicit 'do not be overconfident' calibration step, 85% accuracy","result":"SRC_003 labels this a 'genuine reliability gain' because the expert is biased, but the panel's gain is mere compliance, not genuine reasoning"}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_004","attack":false,"case":""}]}